<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Limits of Polynomials — end behaviour at infinity</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the polynomial \( f(x) = {@fx@} \).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>What is \( \displaystyle\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) \)?</p>
    <div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
  <li><p>What is \( \displaystyle\lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x) \)?</p>
    <div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>For large \(|x|\), the <strong>leading term</strong> \({@p*x^n@}\) dominates every other term, so the end behaviour of \(f\) is the end behaviour of its leading term. The limit is infinite, never a finite number, because the polynomial has degree at least 1.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>As \(x\to\infty\), \(x^{@n@}\) is large and positive, so the sign of the limit is the sign of the leading coefficient \({@p@}\): the limit is [[if test="is(p>0)"]]\(+\infty\)[[else]]\(-\infty\)[[/if]].</p></li>
  <li><p>As \(x\to-\infty\), the parity of the degree matters. If the degree is even, \(x^{n}\) is positive for large negative \(x\), so the behaviour is the same as at \(+\infty\). If the degree is odd, \(x^{n}\) is negative for large negative \(x\), so the sign flips. Here \(n = {@n@}\) is [[if test="n_even"]]even[[else]]odd[[/if]], so the limit is [[if test="neg_is_pos"]]\(+\infty\)[[else]]\(-\infty\)[[/if]].</p></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026063000</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text><![CDATA[p: rand_with_prohib(-5,5,[0]);
q: rand_with_prohib(1,9,[]);
r: rand_with_prohib(1,9,[]);
/* two even and two odd degrees, so parity matters on about half the variants */
n: rand([2,3,4,5]);
n_even: evenp(n);
fx: p*x^n + q*x + r;
/* x -> +infinity: sign follows p. x -> -infinity: same if n even, flipped if n odd. */
lim_pos_inf: if p > 0 then "posinf" else "neginf";
lim_neg_inf: if n_even then lim_pos_inf else (if lim_pos_inf = "posinf" then "neginf" else "posinf");
neg_is_pos: is(lim_neg_inf = "posinf");
/* the other infinity: the parity error at -infinity (same sign when n is odd, flipped when n is even) */
wrong_neg_inf: if lim_neg_inf = "posinf" then "neginf" else "posinf";
opts1: [["posinf", is(lim_pos_inf = "posinf"), "\\(+\\infty\\)"], ["neginf", is(lim_pos_inf = "neginf"), "\\(-\\infty\\)"], ["finite", false, "a finite number"]];
opts2: [["posinf", is(lim_neg_inf = "posinf"), "\\(+\\infty\\)"], ["neginf", is(lim_neg_inf = "neginf"), "\\(-\\infty\\)"], ["finite", false, "a finite number"]];]]></text>
    </questionvariables>
